> seen these changes:
> =A0- fix less troubles than in vanilla
> =A0- make it have charges
> =A0- make it less reliable
> =A0- let it break after some usage
> =A0- remove it entirely

I considered each of those options before making my recharge-time
change (except for removing it!)
The thinking was that I want the unicorn horn to be a powerful and
reliable way of dealing with status conditions, but I don't want it to
completely negate the existence of status conditions.
- Fixing fewer troubles per use is a pretty trivial change,
particularly since it no longer cures attribute point loss. I don't
think this would have much effect.
- Making it have a limited number of charges is a good option, I
think, but it puts the player more at the mercy of the RNG. I don't
really want to put players in the position of having to backtrack 10
levels to get a new unicorn horn that they have stashed. I think it
would be fair and balanced, but just not really the kind of gameplay I
want to reinforce if I can help it.
- Making it less reliable in general would change its purpose a bit.
If it took say 8 uses on average to cure a status effect, then that
might be ok if you just want to curing yourself after accidentally
drinking a potion of hallucination, but it wouldn't be useful in the
middle of a dangerous battle. Also, I think that it was be a bit of a
chore to have to apply the unicorn several times whenever you needed
it.

So I opted for lower reliability - but only when you are overusing it.
With the recharge system, it can be used in the heat of battle, or any
other time, and it will usually work first go. But because it needs to
a significant amount of turns to recharge, you won't want to use it
every single time a umber hulk looks at you.

So as I said, I'm pretty happy with the idea. I figure now it's just a
matter balancing the numbers.



